# Every letter goes to the filter.
#
# On a list screen there is no such thing as a shortcut key. `p` pins a board,
# and pinning is the only thing `p` does -- but on a list it is a letter, and
# it has to reach the filter like any other. Guarding it on "nothing typed yet"
# is the version that looks right and is not: nothing is typed at the start of
# every search, so the guard eats the first letter of the word you meant.
#
# That shipped once. No stop beginning with P could be searched for.
cache ../cache.db
date 2026-09-11
time 08:00
offset -04:00
size 100 14

# Into the bus routes, where typing narrows the list.
enter

# A digit narrows to 44 and 48, and backspace widens again.
type 4
backspace

# `p` is a letter here. No route is named for it, so the list empties. That is
# the point. It went to the filter rather than pinning anything.
key p
esc

# A route is found by its name as well as its number. 44 is
# `Billings Bridge <> Hurdman` and 48 is `Hurdman <> Carleton`, so both match a
# word neither of their numbers contains. Matching the number alone would empty
# the list here, and nothing else in the suite types a route name.
type hurdman

# esc clears what was typed before it walks back a level, so this takes two.
esc
esc

# On a board, the same key pins. The status bar names what it pinned, which is
# how the two meanings stay distinguishable at the moment of pressing.
type 44
enter
key p

# A stop is found by its pole number as well as its name. The search screen
# takes a number too, but that is a database query. This is the in-place filter
# on a stop list, which is a different path and had nothing walking it.
#
# 8384 is WALKLEY / BANFF, whose name holds no digits at all.
#
# The step above pinned a board, so the first screen now opens on that pin and
# Bus has moved down a row. Row 0 is never a fixed thing here.
esc
down
enter
type 44
enter

# The direction is named. A bare enter takes whatever the slice sorts first.
type hurdman
enter
type 8384
